#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794} :activity/id #uuid "fd917f34-57d2-5f05-a29a-d33c8b5c96a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166488 :question/id #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557327}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "360dd2dd-b48a-554c-9341-3a3e93d1e334" :step/math-academy-id 44978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168149 :tutorial/content "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n*Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c862ff42-4e5b-5bbb-aed6-3d9760d339b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7770 :tutorial/title "The Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c77b7ad4-9d8d-58f4-bd79-4c0b0b47cfea" :step/math-academy-id 44981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166489 :question/id #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" :question/math-academy-id "e-12491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481411}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c0151bcd-ba24-5681-9ec6-52888fa06a36" :step/math-academy-id 44982 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481408}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"} :step/id #uuid "a1446b8a-1f48-5f9e-b01f-42db997299c2" :step/math-academy-id 44985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168150 :tutorial/content "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\n \n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n*If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b65eeb5f-8a44-55b9-819c-1c6c0d979d1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7778 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "037c5a22-eee3-557c-b683-370d2389ad82" :step/math-academy-id 45031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168151 :tutorial/content "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b890c915-3364-5ac3-b88b-298ed92d9def" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7784 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "32b5b570-9467-5bff-bc3b-29964ed86c09" :step/math-academy-id 45050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790}}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166974}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538051} :activity/id #uuid "119140e4-3b93-53de-ab7b-40456eb040a1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e360105-eb66-5367-814d-689677702f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480975} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f7126-1aec-56cb-a084-4f34d28dafc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481043} {:answer/id #uuid "a79440e4-ebcc-5811-a667-c1e64c7db96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481055} {:answer/id #uuid "c27b400b-e962-57cc-9be7-11f6f93220fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481111} {:answer/id #uuid "d99527b4-de43-5d96-9eda-144ff935847e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480975} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a10b6cc-8155-5c1e-bf70-aecfcd94d2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b9226c2-67e0-5858-9c64-ae46c4147a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-163257" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$ on the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3418e5df-6b11-57d2-bb99-1913431eb137" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538057}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7b96a3-3ee8-51d8-bf7e-f10bc137dc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480796} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea1906a-4d23-5e92-aa42-2516002c4c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480915} {:answer/id #uuid "654022a5-61f4-581f-8b07-c11340a40a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480925} {:answer/id #uuid "e98d6b52-9796-58a4-ba09-031961cb2020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481186} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2a60a5-710d-5d16-938e-a0bd54aeac9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480925} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8ec83a-e269-5c73-bf54-b07a27aefc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bbfd9077-09aa-55c5-a7ec-ce238f7922af" :question/math-academy-id "q-163240" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2x\\}$ given that the area of the region is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "876ec9b0-fca4-5f08-ab51-9230e03dee9c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1ea06c-900b-518b-90c1-188a82af18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163902} {:answer/id #uuid "4486d7e0-2a87-59a4-9e31-7eb1fae5055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163904} {:answer/id #uuid "3243821a-d8ad-5dd7-aced-e15740550519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480836} {:answer/id #uuid "932abe94-bffd-5dad-a100-ed7dd0878970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481015} {:answer/id #uuid "a461f542-25f5-59e6-a3ba-329bd57b7ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481015} :answer-field/id #uuid "a885e1f2-0379-5509-836d-b0ab6acba537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7ccaa25-bf49-56f7-93ee-b9d797d03126" :question/math-academy-id "q-163068" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd571a32-8693-5c5f-99bb-0070c9bb2fcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27060319-319a-5c93-87a4-021b464e69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278938} {:answer/id #uuid "96cddbff-aa96-5c15-b451-85bc3b5899ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279926} {:answer/id #uuid "d6297845-0a0a-51e3-9056-3d94de569441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280482} {:answer/id #uuid "d932caf8-3377-5328-b82d-2c72ffe1d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280511} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6871d0-cf0d-5786-9a5c-fd24e0463ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf3c23-4651-531a-8ea1-05778a87081e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4da84d34-65b6-5264-9689-a8f568b0485b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163278" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb0bae8e-56e4-5d43-8dbc-3436ea5c01f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9c1a55-bba0-56ad-b34a-3f8e8d7f257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278854} {:answer/id #uuid "626dc16f-8c1c-5295-aa50-90b66258e711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279486} {:answer/id #uuid "67ad624f-1636-5880-94a3-08d7d29a2390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279534} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e6422b-62b6-59e0-932d-a0c2aeb5b3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280446} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cb2768-4bcd-5b37-aeef-50e75f09d3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279486} :answer-field/id #uuid "08429d71-a216-5cf2-b30e-790877dfd6fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94920c42-c29f-5248-bff0-1bbc9f005b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-163246" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {1}^{2}+8\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f52a63f9-5aaf-58b5-bc92-454c75b1e996" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538006}}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537205}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557369} :activity/id #uuid "99747c81-e556-573e-9a49-314528507d7a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aec11462-7527-5c1f-bee1-5311de75df3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163877} {:answer/id #uuid "28a5d564-3fad-5bcd-bbd4-8313b0c26770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163878} {:answer/id #uuid "41a558e9-c044-57fc-99da-3a88324c2b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163879} {:answer/id #uuid "5336652c-5f4b-557f-994b-7062409121b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163880} {:answer/id #uuid "667f6b41-1da3-5dde-85af-5f6ba8248df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163880} :answer-field/id #uuid "91ed5a91-8a47-5da8-b4fa-8f64aaacc1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a40ddcf2-e6e2-5966-908e-b51c9f5915b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163060" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}+3xy]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}18{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[6{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 48 & =(2a+3\\cdot 1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 8 & =2a+3 \\\\ 5 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{5}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{5}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2fdac4bf-88bd-5b8c-8156-abdd95028924" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305e3d19-c9fb-5ff4-8bea-3ba01ec37d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279015} {:answer/id #uuid "553dbfe6-1300-5f62-9d6e-2745efdb74e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279369} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca44dc-1a03-569e-8017-76f798ba4acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279872} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7bea68-5a2c-568c-9b79-3781c74a681b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280257} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbef370-76d9-5b05-9bcd-73012f686620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280797} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ca4de9-3198-5afe-9619-0e002b132fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "185b68b4-cdef-58dd-9006-867ebfafd06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163059" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}+y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+12x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+12\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(1,b)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =({1}^{2}+2b+1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =12b+12 \\\\ 20 & =12b \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,\\frac{5}{3})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{5}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "76a6bdfb-b2d3-5c1f-94fe-835169048009" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a00aa3-d6d0-54f6-84c3-a84dbc1e7955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556539} {:answer/id #uuid "9affac74-7d2b-5cbc-a27c-c731e04fc635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556805} {:answer/id #uuid "b26cb4c0-36e4-5059-b498-2f505edf4153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556869} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f175b1-b2d8-5ccc-8542-dafe46581974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556878} {:answer/id #uuid "e442d8f5-617c-59ab-a4ac-822779b6d576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556878} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dce9295-adc4-54f0-85bb-a7daa031279d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6236dff-5836-5e2b-be74-a41334b247e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163156" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}6xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[12{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =(6a\\cdot 2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 12 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "837149fd-427b-5257-8438-799b8410b152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557383}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557383 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b5a5360-ed9f-5a7e-9e52-047728bc73d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279432} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee39258-8e4f-57f7-a350-4850b92f4855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280004} {:answer/id #uuid "b98bfa68-5b2a-5844-b047-3d433642ead1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280224} {:answer/id #uuid "d87db86d-f745-5889-b660-e63f2c4d38e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280504} {:answer/id #uuid "f5377fd4-0439-592f-84f9-3e1c47801136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280504} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b2d4cbb-97db-5842-8cbf-0379ee6eb626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6b96aef-d640-5c31-9a3e-e83b8daeccf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163206" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+2y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a+2\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 4 & =2a+4 \\\\ 0 & =2a \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(0,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "99580cac-da4a-5eeb-8345-69ac73a2ebdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e3253af-36cf-50a6-b6a4-e0cebd8a088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279300} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb31ddb-172c-5405-bfcb-0893d02422b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d136b5-8a1b-538d-932f-235fc197906b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280044} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c2097d-3ba9-5baa-934e-6dd265730b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280216} {:answer/id #uuid "c944cd9a-1153-596c-ae76-dc3d60055c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279460} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d2b3a3-7b86-546b-8f02-ed9d3ed11cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33038417-f976-5b04-ba1c-849f23418261" :question/math-academy-id "q-163207" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9x}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot (3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\cdot (3)}{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =f(2,b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =(2+b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =9b+18 \\\\ 9 & =9b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c19cb2ee-7084-5301-85f3-3815cc4bddab"}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556415}]}
